title: Why sync state lives outside the data model
tags: Sync
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: Links and snapshots are bookkeeping of a pair, not data of either repository: kept in their own store, they stay out of queries, the event log and rollback — while the plan, whose point is to be browsed, is a repository.

An earlier design made a link a metarecord shared by both repositories, owned by
two databases at once. It was dropped (with the multi-owner model, `6357e5b`): links
and snapshots describe the relation between two repositories, not either of them,
and as metarecords they would have shown in queries, entered the event log and been
moved by rollbacks of one side only.

So they live in a store of their own ([[The sync database]]): the repositories'
queries, indexes and logs never see them, and an undo never makes a link point
nowhere. What a rollback does to a linked record is just another change to that
record — its version says so ([[Change detection in sync]]).

The plan goes the other way, deliberately: it ''is'' a repository ([[The sync plan]]),
because reviewing and editing it is its purpose, and queries, `mf … field set`, the
log and the GUI's panels then come for free. It is isolated in its own system
repository, so it adds nothing to the two synced ones.
